(b) No: A is across part of the circuit / not strictly in series with $R$ alone; low-$R$
ammeter in that position shorts / diverts current. Correct: open the $R$ branch and put A
in series in that branch only.
}

\noindent\textbf{Worked method.}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em]
  \item To measure current \emph{through $R$}, the ammeter must sit in the \textbf{same single path}
        as $R$ (series with $R$), so every coulomb through $R$ also through A.
  \item \textbf{(a)} $R$ and the bulb are in parallel. A is before the split, so it reads the
        sum of both branch currents, not $I_R$ alone. Wrong for Mary's aim.
  \item \textbf{(b)} A bridges the supply lead into $R$ (low resistance path around / across
        that connection). That is not series with $R$, can damage the meter, and does not
        measure $I_R$. Wrong.
\end{itemize}

Correct pattern for ``current through $R$ only'':
\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.0, transform shape]
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1] (0,2.2)
        -- (1.5,2.2)
        to[R, l=$R$] (3.2,2.2)
        to[ammeter, l=A] (4.8,2.2)
        -- (4.8,0) -- (0,0);
  \draw (1.5,2.2) -- (1.5,0.9) to[bulb] (4.8,0.9);
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (2.4,-0.5) {A in series with $R$'s branch only};
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}

