\subsection{4.4 Potential divider}

Two (or more) resistors in series share the supply p.d.\ in proportion to their resistances.
The junction between them is often used to \textbf{tap} a fraction of the supply voltage.

\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.05, transform shape]
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1, l=$V$] (0,2.4)
        to[R, l=$R_1$, v=$V_1$] (2.6,2.4)
        to[R, l=$R_2$, v=$V_2$] (5.2,2.4)
        -- (5.2,0) -- (0,0);
  \node[circ] at (2.6,2.4) {};
  \node[font=\scriptsize,above] at (2.6,2.55) {$P$};
  \draw[-{Latex}] (2.6,2.75) -- (2.6,3.15)
    node[above,font=\scriptsize] {$V_{\mathrm{out}}=V_2$};
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}

\formulabox{%
$V_{\mathrm{out}}=V\dfrac{R_2}{R_1+R_2}
\qquad
V_1=V\dfrac{R_1}{R_1+R_2}$
}

A \textbf{variable resistor} (rheostat) is a potential divider in disguise: the sliding contact
picks off the p.d.\ across one section of the wire.

\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.05, transform shape]
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1, l=$6\,\mathrm{V}$] (0,2.4);
  \draw (0,2.4) -- (0.8,2.4) -- (0.8,0.4)
        to[R, l=$10\,\Omega$] (3.0,0.4)
        -- (3.0,0) -- (0,0);
  \draw (0.8,2.4) to[R, l_=$50\,\Omega$] (4.5,2.4);
  \draw (0.8,0.4) -- (4.5,0.4) -- (4.5,2.4);
  \node[circ] at (1.8,2.4) {};
  \node[font=\scriptsize,above] at (1.8,2.55) {$X$};
  \node[circ] at (3.4,2.4) {};
  \node[font=\scriptsize,above] at (3.4,2.55) {$Y$};
  \node[circ] at (4.5,2.4) {};
  \node[font=\scriptsize,above] at (4.5,2.55) {$Z$};
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (3.0,-0.35) {bulb};
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em,itemsep=0.12em,topsep=0.25em]
  \item $R_{XY}$ and the bulb are in series across $6\,\mathrm{V}$.
  \item Moving the contact changes $R_{XY}:R_{YZ}$, so the bulb current changes.
  \item Increasing a series rheostat: total $R\uparrow\Rightarrow I\downarrow$.
        The voltmeter across the fixed load usually falls; across the rheostat it usually rises.
\end{itemize}

\question
Bob connects a $10\,\Omega$ bulb and a variable resistor ($0$--$50\,\Omega$) in series with a
$6\,\mathrm{V}$ cell, as shown above. With the sliding contact at $Y$, the bulb draws
$0.3\,\mathrm{A}$.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Find the ratio $XY:YZ$.
  \item The resistance wire $XZ$ is $50\,\mathrm{cm}$ long with radius $1\,\mathrm{mm}$.
        Find the resistivity of the wire.
\end{enumerate}
\sol[3.5cm]{%
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $R_{XY}+R_{\mathrm{bulb}}=V/I=6/0.3=20\,\Omega\Rightarrow R_{XY}=10\,\Omega$.
        $XY:YZ=10:40=1:4$.
  \item $\rho=RA/\ell=(50)\pi(0.001)^{2}/0.5=3.14\times 10^{-4}\,\Omega\,\mathrm{m}$.
\end{enumerate}
}

\question
$6\,\mathrm{V}$ and $12\,\mathrm{V}$ cells oppose through $2\,\Omega$ and $4\,\Omega$ in series.
Find $V_{PQ}$ between the mid-junctions of the two branches.
\sol[2.8cm]{%
Net e.m.f.\ $|12-6|=6\,\mathrm{V}$, $R_{\mathrm{eq}}=6\,\Omega$, $I=1\,\mathrm{A}$.
Then $V_{PQ}$ from drops around either path: often $0\,\mathrm{V}$ if the mid-points sit at the
same potential.
}

