\section{Ammeters and voltmeters}

Series/parallel meter connections and ideal $R$ were introduced in \S1.3 and \S2.6.
This section covers wiring mistakes, non-ideal meters, and exam technique.

\remark{Exam default: treat meters as ideal unless the question states otherwise.}

\conceptcheck{%
  An ideal voltmeter should have
  \begin{enumerate}[label=\Alph*.,itemsep=0.05em,topsep=0.25em,leftmargin=1.8em]
    \item very small resistance
    \item very large resistance
    \item resistance equal to the component under test
  \end{enumerate}}
% answer: B

\subsection{How to wire the meters}

Ammeter: break the wire and insert it in series (correct polarity: $+$ of meter toward battery
$+$). Voltmeter: bridge across the two points of interest in parallel (again match polarity).

\question
Mary wants to measure the current passing through the \emph{resistor} in each set-up.
Are the ammeters correctly connected?
\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=0.95, transform shape]
  \node[font=\small\bfseries] at (1.8,3.0) {(a)};
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1] (0,2.2)
        to[ammeter, l=A] (2.0,2.2)
        -- (3.6,2.2) -- (3.6,0) -- (0,0);
  \draw (2.0,2.2) to[R, l=$R$] (2.0,0);
  \draw (3.6,2.2) to[bulb] (3.6,0);
\end{circuitikz}
\qquad
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=0.95, transform shape]
  \node[font=\small\bfseries] at (1.8,3.0) {(b)};
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1] (0,2.2)
        -- (2.0,2.2) to[R, l=$R$] (3.8,2.2)
        to[bulb] (3.8,0) -- (0,0);
  % ammeter wrongly across the feed wire (almost a short)
  \draw (0.5,2.2) to[ammeter, l=A] (0.5,0.8) -- (2.0,0.8) -- (2.0,2.2);
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}
\sol{%
(a) No for ``through $R$ only'': $R\parallel$ bulb, A is in the main line $\Rightarrow$ reads
$total$ current $I_R+I_{\mathrm{bulb}}$.
(b) No: A is across part of the circuit / not strictly in series with $R$ alone; low-$R$
ammeter in that position shorts / diverts current. Correct: open the $R$ branch and put A
in series in that branch only.
}

\subsection{Real (non-ideal) meters}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.95\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Effect \\
\midrule
Ammeter has small but non-zero $R_A$: it slightly reduces the true current. Reading
$I_A < I_0$ (current without the meter). \\
Voltmeter has large but finite $R_V$: it draws a tiny current and slightly reduces the p.d.\
across the component. Reading $V_V < V_0$. \\
Voltmeter current is usually negligible because $R_V$ is very large (not because it is ``ohmic''). \\
Extending a voltmeter range: series multiplier resistor
$R_{\mathrm{m}}=(V_{\mathrm{full}}/I_{\mathrm{fsd}})-R_{\mathrm{meter}}$. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\subsection{Voltmeter between named points}

Moving one voltmeter lead from $Y$ to $Z$ changes which path p.d.\ you measure.
If both switches open or both closed leave the meter still across the full cell, the reading can
stay $\mathcal{E}$. If a short joins the two probes, the reading is $0$.

\subsection{Which circuit measures $R$ correctly?}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.95\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Idea \\
\midrule
Wanted: $R=V/I$ for the component. \\
If $R$ is comparable to the ammeter resistance, put the voltmeter \emph{across the component
only} (ammeter outside the voltmeter loop); otherwise the ammeter's $Ir$ is included in $V$. \\
If $R$ is very large, put the ammeter in series with the component and the voltmeter across both
(voltmeter current then matters less relative to $I$). \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

