\section{Power and energy}

\subsection{5.1 Energy transformed per unit time}

\textbf{Power} $P$ is the rate at which energy is transformed (energy transferred per unit time):
\formulabox{$\displaystyle P=\frac{E}{t}$}
For a circuit component,
\formulabox{$\displaystyle P=VI=I^{2}R=\frac{V^{2}}{R}$}
Unit: watt (W), where $1\,\mathrm{W}=1\,\mathrm{J\,s^{-1}}=1\,\mathrm{V\cdot A}$.
In a resistor, electrical energy is usually converted mainly to heat.

\conceptcheck{%
  Resistors $R$ and $2R$ are connected in turn across the same fixed supply $V$. Which dissipates
  more power?
  \begin{enumerate}[label=\Alph*.,itemsep=0.05em,topsep=0.25em,leftmargin=1.8em]
    \item $R$
    \item $2R$
    \item they dissipate equally
  \end{enumerate}}
% answer: A ($P=V^{2}/R$; smaller $R$ $\Rightarrow$ larger $P$ at fixed $V$)

At fixed supply voltage, smaller $R$ dissipates more power ($P=V^{2}/R$).
At fixed current, larger $R$ dissipates more power ($P=I^{2}R$).

\question
A $12\,\mathrm{V}$ battery of negligible internal resistance supplies $2.0\,\mathrm{A}$ to a heater for
$5.0$~minutes.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Find the power.
  \item Find the energy transferred.
\end{enumerate}
\sol[3.8cm]{%
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $P=VI=(12)(2.0)=24\,\mathrm{W}$.
  \item $E=Pt=24\times(5.0\times 60)=7200\,\mathrm{J}$.
\end{enumerate}
}

\subsection{5.2 Short circuit}

An \textbf{ideal wire} has $R=0$: every point on it is at the same potential (same colour).

A \textbf{short circuit} is a zero-$R$ path joining two points that were at different potentials.
The ends become equipotential; any component only \emph{between} those ends is bypassed.

\question
\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.1, transform shape]
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1, l=$12\,\mathrm{V}$] (0,2.6)
        to[R, l=$4\,\Omega$] (2.6,2.6)
        to[R, l=$4\,\Omega$] (5.2,2.6)
        -- (5.2,0) -- (0,0);
  \draw (2.6,2.6) node[above,font=\scriptsize] {$X$}
        to[R, l_=$8\,\Omega$] (2.6,0);
  \draw[thick] (2.6,2.6) -- (3.5,2.6) -- (3.5,0) -- (2.6,0);
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (3.9,1.3) {ideal short};
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}
An ideal short joins $X$ to the bottom rail. Find the current from the cell.
\sol{%
Short forces $X$ onto the bottom-rail colour.
$8\,\Omega$ and right $4\,\Omega$ bypassed $\Rightarrow$ $I=12/4=3.0\,\mathrm{A}$.
}

