%======================================================================
\section{Practical cells and meters}

Ideal cells and meters were assumed in \S1--\S5. Real apparatus has internal resistance and
finite meter resistances that can alter the circuit under test.

\remark{Exam default: treat meters as ideal unless the question states otherwise.}

\subsection{6.1 Practical cells}

A real cell is modelled as e.m.f.\ $\mathcal{E}$ in series with a small \textbf{internal
resistance} $r$ (inside the dashed box of the cell).

For an external load $R$,
\[
I=\frac{\mathcal{E}}{R+r},\qquad
V=\mathcal{E}-Ir=\frac{\mathcal{E}R}{R+r},
\]
where $V$ is the terminal p.d.\ across the load. A fraction $Ir$ is lost across $r$ inside the cell.

Open circuit ($R\to\infty$): $I=0$, so the terminal reading is $V\approx\mathcal{E}$.
Short across the terminals ($R=0$): $I_{\mathrm{sc}}=\mathcal{E}/r$ (can be very large).

\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.1, transform shape]
  \draw[dashed] (-0.45,-0.35) rectangle (3.55,2.35);
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.55,2.65) {real cell};
  \draw (0,1.5) to[battery1, l={$\mathcal{E}$}] (1.5,1.5)
        to[R, l=$r$, resistors/scale=0.55] (3.0,1.5);
  \draw (3.0,1.5) to[short, -o] (3.8,1.5);
  \draw (0,1.5) -- (0,0) -- (3.8,0) node[ocirc]{};
  \draw (3.8,1.5) to[R, l=$R$] (3.8,0);
  \draw[{Latex}-{Latex},thick] (4.35,1.5) -- (4.35,0)
    node[midway,right,font=\scriptsize,align=left]
    {terminal\\voltage\\$V=\mathcal{E}-Ir$};
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}

To raise terminal voltage $V=\mathcal{E}R/(R+r)$: increase external $R$ (or reduce $I$).
A variable-load experiment plotting $V$ vs $I$ gives slope magnitude $=r$ and intercept $=\mathcal{E}$.

\worked{2012}{28}{B}{42}{../questions/2012_q28.png}{%
External: $3\,\Omega\parallel 6\,\Omega$ gives $2\,\Omega$.
Total $R+r=2+2=4\,\Omega$, so $I_{\mathrm{tot}}=12/4=3\,\mathrm{A}$.
Terminal $V=12-3\cdot 2=6\,\mathrm{V}$.
Current in $6\,\Omega$: $I_6=6/6=1.0\,\mathrm{A}$ $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{B}.}

\worked{2014}{24}{C}{20}{../questions/2014_q24.png}{%
$S$ closed: $3\,\Omega$ shorted, external $R=6\,\Omega$, $I=3.0\,\mathrm{A}$.
$\mathcal{E}=3(6+r)=18+3r$.
$S$ open: external $R'=9\,\Omega$, $I'=(18+3r)/(9+r)$.
For $r>0$: $2.0\,\mathrm{A}<I'<3.0\,\mathrm{A}$. Only $2.4\,\mathrm{A}$ fits $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{C}.}

\subsection{6.2 Ammeters and voltmeters in practice}

Series/parallel connections are in \S1.3 and \S2.6. Here: wiring mistakes, loading, and
systematic errors when meters are \emph{not} ideal.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.95\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Effect \\
\midrule
Ammeter has small but non-zero $R_A$: slightly reduces the true current ($I_A<I_0$). \\
Voltmeter has large but finite $R_V$: draws a small current and slightly lowers the p.d.\
across the component ($V_V<V_0$). \\
Choose $R_V\gg$ the resistance across which you measure; choose $R_A\ll$ the series
resistance in that branch. \\
Extending a voltmeter range: series multiplier
$R_{\mathrm{m}}=(V_{\mathrm{full}}/I_{\mathrm{fsd}})-R_{\mathrm{meter}}$. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\conceptcheck{%
  An ideal voltmeter should have
  \begin{enumerate}[label=\Alph*.,itemsep=0.05em,topsep=0.25em,leftmargin=1.8em]
    \item very small resistance
    \item very large resistance
    \item resistance equal to the component under test
  \end{enumerate}}
% answer: B

\conceptcheck{%
  T/F: a high-resistance voltmeter connected alone across a cell must read less than
  $\mathcal{E}$, because every cell has internal resistance.}
% answer: False ($I\approx 0$ so $Ir\approx 0$; reading $\approx\mathcal{E}$)

\textbf{Voltmeter loading.} Two $10\,\mathrm{k}\Omega$ resistors in series across $6\,\mathrm{V}$
share $3\,\mathrm{V}$ each. A $10\,\mathrm{k}\Omega$ voltmeter in parallel with one resistor
forms $5\,\mathrm{k}\Omega\parallel 10\,\mathrm{k}\Omega=3.3\,\mathrm{k}\Omega$, so the
reading drops well below $3\,\mathrm{V}$. With $100\,\Omega$ resistors the same voltmeter
draws a much smaller \emph{fraction} of the branch current, so the reading stays close to
$3\,\mathrm{V}$.

\question
Mary wants to measure the current passing through the \emph{resistor} in each set-up.
Are the ammeters correctly connected?
\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=0.95, transform shape]
  \node[font=\small\bfseries] at (1.8,3.0) {(a)};
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1] (0,2.2)
        to[ammeter, l=A] (2.0,2.2)
        -- (3.6,2.2) -- (3.6,0) -- (0,0);
  \draw (2.0,2.2) to[R, l=$R$] (2.0,0);
  \draw (3.6,2.2) to[bulb] (3.6,0);
\end{circuitikz}
\qquad
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=0.95, transform shape]
  \node[font=\small\bfseries] at (1.8,3.0) {(b)};
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1] (0,2.2)
        -- (2.0,2.2) to[R, l=$R$] (3.8,2.2)
        to[bulb] (3.8,0) -- (0,0);
  \draw (0.5,2.2) to[ammeter, l=A] (0.5,0.8) -- (2.0,0.8) -- (2.0,2.2);
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}
\sol{%
(a) No for ``through $R$ only'': $R\parallel$ bulb; A is in the main line $\Rightarrow$ reads
total current $I_R+I_{\mathrm{bulb}}$.
(b) No: A is not in series with $R$ alone; the low-$R$ ammeter diverts current. Open the $R$
branch and insert A in series there.
}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.95\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Measuring resistance $R=V/I$ \\
\midrule
If $R$ is comparable to $R_A$, put the voltmeter \emph{across the component only} (ammeter
outside the voltmeter loop); otherwise the ammeter's $Ir$ is included in $V$. \\
If $R$ is very large, put the ammeter in series with the component and the voltmeter across
both (voltmeter current matters less relative to $I$). \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

